\ A definition claiming `override` over an imported name wins the frame binding, and a runtime
\ redefinition of that name then takes the own-word arity path rather than the import-conflict
\ guard. In a binary this pins the seed order: the entry-word seed runs after the imports are
\ restored, so the overriding definition's binding is what the redefinition finds, exactly as
\ interpreted.
\
\ The bare call is the file's last statement, so the raise stays in tail position on every tier.

use { taken: } "def_over_import_mod.1z" ;

taken: override ( -- s ) [ "mine" ] ;

taken print-line

redefine-own: ( -- ) [ taken: ( x -- s ) [ drop "B" ] ; ] ;

redefine-own
